                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Structure**\n\nI'm currently focused on dissecting the structure of the input and output grids. They're 8x8, organized into 3x3 blocks. Each block is essentially two 2x2 colored regions (blue) separated by black (0) entries, which is something I want to represent systematically. I am now formalizing the row and block structure in my mind.\n\n\n**Formalizing Block Indexing**\n\nI've formalized a block indexing scheme: (R, C) where R and C range from 0 to 2. This helps to locate each 2x2 block within the 8x8 grid. I've also defined relative coordinates within the blocks, using (dr, dc) as {0, 1} x {0, 1}. Furthermore, I've observed patterns in the input and output, such as how the center block receives data if the endpoints have that data at the same relative position. Now trying to analyze the edge cases.\n\n\n**Investigating Center Block Dynamics**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the behavior of center blocks (1,1). Observing that center blocks, such as in the example, often seem to inherit data from opposite outer blocks in a row or column at the same relative position, I'm trying to solidify these patterns. However, I'm now curious whether this holds true for all middle blocks. Specifically, I am trying to determine if the rule of data propagation applies strictly to the center block, or if other \"middle\" blocks also follow this behavior, or not.\n\n\n",
